.TH TOUCH 3
.SH NAME
touch \- the Raspberry Pi touch display's raw touch buffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #T /dev

.B /dev/touch
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The official Raspberry Pi DSI display's touch controller, an FT5406, is
polled by the VideoCore firmware, which keeps the controller's register
block in a buffer.
.I Touch
serves that buffer as one read-only file.
.PP
Each read of
.B touch
returns one whole 64-byte snapshot, and marks it consumed in the way the
firmware expects, by setting the point count to 99; the firmware
overwrites that at its next poll.
A snapshot whose point count is 99 is therefore one that has been read
already.
.PP
The layout is the panel's and is not interpreted here:
.IP
.EX
.ta 8n
[0]	device mode
[1]	gesture
[2]	number of points, 0-10; 99 if unchanged since the last read
[3+6i]	bits 7:6 event, bits 3:0 high nibble of x
[4+6i]	low byte of x
[5+6i]	bits 7:4 touch id, bits 3:0 high nibble of y
[6+6i]	low byte of y
[7+6i]	weight;  [8+6i] area
.EE
.PP
.B /dis/touch.dis
.RB ( /os/init/touch.b ),
which
.IR osinit (8)
starts, reads it and writes pointer events to
.B /dev/pointer
(see
.IR pointer (3)).
What is in the kernel is what a program cannot do: ask the firmware for
the buffer, reach memory the firmware shares, and keep the cache honest
about it.
.PP
The device refuses to attach when there is no panel, so the bind fails and
there is no file.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2837/devtouch.c
.br
.B /os/init/touch.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pointer (3),
.IR osinit (8)
.SH BUGS
Raspberry Pi only.
QEMU implements neither of the firmware requests involved, so the device
has been exercised only on a board.
